var e={resourceClusters:[{key:`AEO Fundamentals`,blurb:`Foundational guides on what AEO is, how it differs from SEO, and where it fits inside an existing content workflow.`},{key:`Strategy & Positioning`,blurb:`Manifesto-style essays on why AI search is a decision layer, what Google's May 2026 generative AI guide changes for AEO, and where llms.txt fits in the priority stack.`},{key:`How SolCrys Works`,blurb:`How SolCrys measures visibility, diagnoses answer gaps, uses Corporate Context to keep actions brand-safe, and verifies movement over time. Includes our editorial standards.`},{key:`For Agencies`,blurb:`How agencies launch AEO services, package audits, and prove action-to-result outcomes for clients.`},{key:`Retail AEO`,blurb:`How brands stay visible, accurate, and recommended inside AI shopping assistants and the new digital shelf.`},{key:`AI Engine Optimization`,blurb:`Engine-specific guides for ChatGPT Search, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews and AI Mode, Claude, and Gemini.`},{key:`Citation & Source Influence`,blurb:`How AI engines choose which sources to cite, and how brands earn presence in the editorial, community, and review sources that shape buyer answers.`},{key:`Community & UGC`,blurb:`How Reddit, LinkedIn, YouTube, product Q&A coverage, and comments show up as AI citations — and the ethical playbooks for earning them.`},{key:`Measurement`,blurb:`How we measure mentions, citations, share of recommendation, and competitor presence across answer engines.`},{key:`Attribution & ROI`,blurb:`How to build the AEO business case, attribute revenue to AI-influenced visits, and measure whether shipped actions actually moved answers.`},{key:`Prompt Intelligence`,blurb:`How to design and operate the prompt sets that drive AEO visibility, audits, and retesting.`},{key:`Technical Readiness`,blurb:`How crawler access, structured data, and answer-ready content connect technical SEO to AEO outcomes.`},{key:`MCP & Skills`,blurb:`How AI agents read your SolCrys workspace via the MCP server, and the open-source Skills extension that turns visibility data into CMO briefs, gap analyses, and publish-ready drafts.`},{key:`Risk Monitoring`,blurb:`How to monitor AI hallucinations, accuracy risk, and brand-sensitive answers across engines.`},{key:`Buyer Guides`,blurb:`Vendor evaluation frameworks, architecture comparisons, stack-decision matrices, and pilot playbooks for choosing the right AEO platform.`},{key:`Competitor Comparisons`,blurb:`Compare SolCrys with adjacent AI visibility and AEO platforms by buyer fit, workflow depth, data coverage, and execution model.`}],resourcePages:[{slug:`agentic-web-identity-vs-capability`,title:`Identity and Capability: The Agentic Web Still Runs on Being Chosen`,description:`The agentic web is splitting into an identity layer and a capability layer. Neither fires until an answer engine chooses your brand first. Here is the order of operations, and why AEO is the precondition for both.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-07-13`,summary:`The agentic web is splitting into two layers: identity, which tells a model who you are, and capability, which lets an agent complete a task on your site. Both matter. But both assume an answer engine already routed a user to you. The layer that decides that, whether an engine names and cites your brand at all, comes first. And it is the one you can measure today.`},{slug:`aeo-for-investors`,title:`AEO for Investors: Diligence Now Starts with an AI Answer`,description:`Investors now run market maps, competitor scans, and first-pass diligence through ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Copilot. If AI doesn't know your startup exists, or describes it wrong, you can be screened out before the first meeting. How to audit and fix what answer engines say about your company before you raise.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-07-07`,summary:`Your next investor has already researched your startup before they've opened your deck. The first pass of diligence now happens inside AI assistants: scanning competitors, mapping the category, checking whether a company is legitimate, all before a partner meeting even lands on your calendar. That makes fundraising readiness an Answer Engine Optimization problem. And if ChatGPT gets your company wrong, or has never heard of it, investors quietly rule you out, and you never find out why. I hear this from founders heading into a raise every month, so this guide lays out what our research shows about how investors use AI, the five ways startups lose it, a six-prompt self-audit to run before your raise, and how to fix what answer engines say about your company.`},{slug:`ai-citation-first-mover-window`,title:`The AI Citation Window Is Closing: Why AEO Timing Beats Tactics`,description:`The sources an AI trusts for a category narrow over time toward two or three brands. While that pool is still open, getting cited is cheap; once it freezes around incumbents, displacing an anchor costs far more. So AEO has a first-mover dynamic, and the strategic question isn't only what to do, it's whether your category's citation window is still open.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`e-e-a-t-for-ai-search`,title:`The New E-E-A-T: Where Experience, Expertise, Authority & Trust Actually Live in AI Search`,description:`E-E-A-T isn't dead in AI search, it moved. The four qualities still decide whether AI recommends you, but they're no longer signaled on your own page, they're established across the sources the model trusts. How each of experience, expertise, authoritativeness, and trust relocates for AEO.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`owned-earned-paid-ai-visibility`,title:`Owned, Earned, Paid: How Your Media Mix Maps to AI Visibility (and Why the Hierarchy Flips)`,description:`The owned/earned/paid split you use for marketing maps onto AI visibility with one twist: the hierarchy flips. In an AI answer, trust runs inverse to control, the sources you own are trusted least, the independent ones you can't buy are trusted most. A map of each layer and where to focus.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`do-sponsored-articles-move-ai-answers`,title:`Do Sponsored Articles Move AI Answers? What Paid Placements on TechTarget, Forbes & CIO Actually Buy You`,description:`A sponsored article on TechTarget, Forbes, or CIO.com can change what AI says about you, but not the way a paid-media plan assumes. It works only when the domain is one the model already cites and the content reads as editorial, not an ad. Then it's a corroboration asset, not impressions. How to tell the difference, and measure it.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`your-best-proof-is-private`,title:`Your Best Proof Is Trapped in Private AI Chats. Here's How to Make It Public.`,description:`There's a specific reason your 'why we're the best' page does nothing in AI answers, and why a great specialist product stays invisible: the model discounts what you say about yourself, and your real proof happens in private chats and emails it can't read. How to move the private conclusion your customers already reached into public, in their words.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`aeo-for-pr`,title:`AEO for PR: How to Prove Your Earned Media Actually Moved the AI Answer`,description:`Earned media is the single most powerful AEO lever a brand has, because it's the corroboration layer AI trusts, but PR measurement wasn't built to see it. How comms teams measure whether a campaign, announcement, or media hit actually changed what AI says, with a clean before/after.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`why-ai-ignores-your-star-rating`,title:`Your Star Rating Gets You Shown in AI Answers. Your Review Text Gets You Picked.`,description:`Star ratings reach AI answers through licensed or first-party data, such as Yelp in ChatGPT. Review text explains the pick. How to earn both within the rules.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`ai-citation-variance-spend-decision`,title:`Contested, Settling, or Decaying? Turn AI-Citation Variance Into a Spend Decision`,description:`The contested-vs-settled signal tells you which AI queries are winnable. This is how to act on it: measure the noise floor per query, classify each query's phase, route each phase to a different move, and avoid the trap that wrecks the whole thing, mistaking a query that's dying for one you're losing.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-06-28`},{slug:`ai-source-incentive-map`,title:`AI Doesn't Cite a Neutral Web. It Cites an Incentive Map.`,description:`AI leans on third-party sources to decide what's true about your brand, but those sources aren't neutral. Each one carries an incentive that predicts the way it bends the truth about you. How to read the source set behind your AI answers as an incentive map, and win the sources whose incentive aligns with you.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`schema-markup-for-ai-search`,title:`Schema Markup for AI Search: What Structured Data Actually Does (and Where It Stops)`,description:`Schema markup helps AI engines parse and classify your brand, and makes you eligible for richer treatment. It does not, on its own, get you cited or trusted. The honest, complete guide to structured data for AEO: what it does, what it doesn't, which types matter, and how to ship it so it moves the answer.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`fix-wrong-fact-in-ai-answer`,title:`How to Fix a Wrong Fact in an AI Answer About Your Brand`,description:`An AI engine is stating a wrong price, a dead feature, or a bad comparison about you, and there's no one to email. The wrong fact is a relayed source. Here's how to find it, fix it or outweigh it, and retest until the answer flips.`,category:`Risk Monitoring`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`is-ai-telling-the-truth-about-your-brand`,title:`When AI Describes Your Brand, Is It Telling the Truth?`,description:`AI answers drop claims you earned, quote prices you retired, and assert things you never said, and that text can be steered on purpose. How to grade every AI answer against your own grounding truth, with receipts.`,category:`Risk Monitoring`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`grounding-depth-ai-brand-accuracy`,title:`Why Grounding Depth Decides Whether AI Gets Your Brand Right`,description:`AI answer accuracy about your brand is only as good as the truth you grade against. A thin baseline context catches crude contradictions; a deep, cited, current source of truth catches drift — and the claims a shallow context is silent about.`,category:`Risk Monitoring`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`managed-corporate-context`,title:`Managed Corporate Context: When an Auto-Generated Baseline Isn't Enough`,description:`SolCrys auto-generates a baseline Corporate Context on every plan — the right start. For multi-brand, multi-region, and regulated companies, the source of truth grounding AI answers has to go deeper — broadly researched, claim-by-claim verified, and kept current. That's Managed Corporate Context.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`answer-accuracy-coverage-gap`,title:`You Can't Grade What Your Context Is Silent About: The Answer-Accuracy Coverage Gap`,description:`An accuracy check can only catch deviations from what your source of truth records — and only on the questions you grade. Here are the two coverage gaps that turn 'all green' into false reassurance, and how to close them.`,category:`Risk Monitoring`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`are-saas-directory-submissions-worth-it-2026`,title:`Are SaaS Directory Submissions Still Worth It in 2026?`,description:`G2, Capterra, and Product Hunt listings pay off only where buyers check them or AI engines cite them for your category. How to test before you submit.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`ai-brand-disambiguation-playbook`,title:`When AI Confuses Your Brand With a Same-Name Company: A Disambiguation Playbook`,description:`An AI engine keeps mixing your brand up with a same-name company and citing both. Here's the four-step entity-disambiguation playbook to separate them — without ever naming the other company on your own site.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-06-15`,summary:`An AI engine that confuses your company with a same-name one is doing entity resolution badly: it merges two real-world entities, cross-contaminates their attributes, and splits your branded-query citations between them. The fix is not to tell it "we're not them." There is no schema property for that, hidden-in-the-backend disambiguation is both a cloaking risk and ignored by AI, and naming the other company only reinforces the link. You win by establishing a distinct, consistently corroborated identity: a canonical entity homepage, a single Organization schema node with a stable @id, consistent sameAs, founder, and location properties, and a category-first disambiguatingDescription, and a corresponding Wikidata item. The single explicit "different from" assertion lives off your own site, on Wikidata (property P1889), so you never have to name a competitor in your own copy. It is a four-step loop — measure the confusion across engines, diagnose the missing signals, execute the positive fixes, and verify the answers actually changed, on each engine's own clock.`},{slug:`testable-geo-playbook`,title:`Most GEO Advice Is Untestable. Here's How to Run It, and Not Fool Yourself.`,description:`AI answers are non-deterministic, so a naive GEO test lies to you confidently. The four test-design rules that decide whether your AI-visibility result is signal or noise: measure a rate, test at the buyer's specificity level, test per language, and sort each cited source by the move.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[`Citation & Source Influence`],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`Most GEO advice is a vibe, so the fix is to treat each claim as a small experiment you can run in an afternoon. But AI answers are non-deterministic, which means a naive experiment lies to you confidently. Four test-design rules decide whether a result is signal or noise. Rule one: measure an appearance rate across roughly ten runs, never a single yes/no, because the same prompt set can swing run to run while the real windowed average sits elsewhere. Rule two: test at the specificity level a buyer actually uses, because a brand visible on a broad prompt can vanish the moment you add a constraint like budget, and that constrained prompt is both where the buyer stands and where it is cheapest to win. Rule three: test per language, because the source map changes completely across languages and a brand invisible in English can be the only clear answer in a lower-competition language. Rule four: sort each cited source by the move, using a competitive-query test, so a flat list of cited domains becomes a list with a move attached to each. Underneath these rules sit the source map, branded-versus-buyer prompts, share of voice, and description accuracy, each covered in its own guide. None of it requires a tool.`},{slug:`how-ai-describes-your-brand`,title:`How AI Updates Its Description of You (and Why a Wrong One Is Worse Than No Mention)`,description:`AI decides what your brand is, and each engine decides differently. How the description forms, why a fix takes weeks to show, and how to correct it.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[`Risk Monitoring`],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`Most AEO advice is about whether AI cites you. The harder problem is what it says you are once it does, and a confident wrong description is worse than no mention because it sets an expectation your signup or sales call can't meet. The description is assembled at retrieval time from the sources each engine trusts, so it differs by engine, and when your canonical page is ambiguous the model fills the gap from adjacent products. A fix doesn't flip the answer instantly: engines that search climb as they re-fetch, Google's AI surfaces follow its index cadence, and answers from model memory change only when the model does. The remedy is a Measure → Diagnose → Execute → Verify loop, judged over weeks, not a single run.`},{slug:`chatgpt-recommends-competitor-self-audit`,title:`Your SaaS Ranks #1 on Google. ChatGPT Recommends a Competitor. The 10-Minute Self-Audit.`,description:`Ranking #1 on Google does not mean AI engines recommend you. Run this 10-minute test across ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, and Google AI Overviews to see whether you are cited, mispositioned, or invisible - with an illustrative worked example of a mid-market vendor that owns its branded prompt but is absent from the buyer prompts that decide deals.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`Google rank and AI recommendation are decoupled. Being indexed is not the same as being cited, and being cited is not the same as being recommended first. A B2B founder can sit at position 1 in Google and still be absent from the ChatGPT, Perplexity, or Gemini answer that a buyer reads before they ever open a search tab. This guide gives you a runnable 10-minute test, a way to read the three failure modes (not cited, cited-but-mispositioned, cited-below-competitor), and an illustrative worked example of a mid-market vendor that owns its branded prompt yet sits at or near zero on the buyer prompts that actually decide deals while a category leader is named first.`},{slug:`is-ai-visibility-a-vanity-metric`,title:`Is AI Visibility Tracking a Vanity Metric? How to Make It a Signal Your CEO Can Trust.`,description:`Your CEO wants AI visibility on the dashboard and your gut says vanity metric. It is - if you track raw rank on vanity prompts. Here are the three conditions that turn it into a leading indicator, a CEO-ready reporting template, and an illustrative 100%-vs-0% scenario that proves the point.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`A Series B growth lead whose CEO returns from a conference asking to 'add AI visibility to the dashboard' has a reasonable instinct: this could be a vanity metric. They are half right. Raw AI 'rank' on a flattering prompt set is a vanity metric. But AI visibility becomes a genuine top-of-funnel leading indicator under three conditions: you measure share of voice on buyer-pain prompts rather than vanity prompts, you report it as a relative position against competitors rather than an absolute number, and you watch directional change and sentiment rather than worshipping the level. This guide gives you those three conditions, a CEO-ready reporting template, and an illustrative scenario where the same brand reads as 100% visible or 0% visible depending entirely on which prompt you point it at.`},{slug:`organic-traffic-died-ai-overviews-recovery`,title:`SaaS Organic Traffic Died in 2026? The AI Overviews Diagnostic and Recovery Playbook.`,description:`Informational traffic falling off a cliff in 2026 is real and measurable - Seer found a 61% organic CTR drop on queries with an AI Overview. Here is the 3-minute Search Console diagnostic, an honest verdict on what is not coming back, and the recovery path the data actually supports.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[`Measurement`],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`Marketers across SaaS are watching informational organic traffic fall and dating it, loosely, to 'January.' The instinct is right; the date is off. The measurable damage lines up with the March 2026 and May 2026 Google core updates layered on top of AI Overviews and the global expansion of AI Mode. The signature is specific: impressions hold roughly flat while clicks fall, because your page is now feeding the AI answer without earning the click. This playbook gives you a 3-minute Search Console diagnostic to confirm it, an honest verdict on which traffic is not coming back, and the recovery path the data supports - being the cited source inside the answer, pivoting toward decision and branded queries, and winning the community source layer - plus the quick fixes to refuse.`},{slug:`survivor-pages-ai-content-template`,title:`The Pages You Were About to Kill Are Your New Content Template`,description:`In the AI-answer era your informational pages weren't penalized, they were absorbed. The pages that survived are your new content brief. One question tells you which.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`answer-engine-optimization`,title:`Answer Engine Optimization`,description:`Answer Engine Optimization makes your brand a source AI engines retrieve, trust, cite, and describe correctly. The two layers it must win, what a 2026 study of 252K citations shows actually drives citations, and the measure-diagnose-execute-verify loop.`,category:`AEO Fundamentals`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`answer-gap-content-brief`,title:`The Answer Gap Is the New Content Brief`,description:`Learn what an AI answer gap is, why it matters for AEO, and how marketing teams can turn weak AI answers into practical content briefs.`,category:`AEO Fundamentals`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-04`},{slug:`corporate-context-ai-marketing`,title:`Corporate Context Is the New CMS`,description:`Corporate Context gives AI marketing agents the brand facts, claims, guardrails, and evidence they need to execute safely across AEO workflows.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-07`},{slug:`ai-visibility-dashboard-vs-aeo-execution-engine`,title:`AI Visibility Dashboard vs AEO Execution Engine`,description:`AEO platforms split into two architectures: dashboards measure and report; execution engines diagnose, fix, and verify. This guide compares 6 use cases, walks through 5 real scenarios, and ends with a decision tree.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`aeo-vs-seo`,title:`AEO vs SEO`,description:`Compare SEO and Answer Engine Optimization, including goals, content formats, measurement, and the role of technical SEO in AI discovery.`,category:`AEO Fundamentals`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`ranking-but-not-cited-in-ai`,title:`Ranking but Not Cited`,description:`You rank well and still aren't cited in AI answers. Here are the five gaps SEO leaves — per-engine citation, corroboration, extractability, accuracy, and measurement — and how to close each.`,category:`AEO Fundamentals`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`ai-brand-visibility-monitoring`,title:`AI Brand Visibility Monitoring`,description:`A practical guide to measuring brand mentions, citations, sentiment, and competitive position across AI answer engines.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`chatgpt-brand-mentions`,title:`ChatGPT Brand Mentions`,description:`Learn how to monitor whether ChatGPT mentions, cites, or misrepresents a brand across high-intent customer prompts.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-04`},{slug:`ai-share-of-recommendation`,title:`AI Share of Recommendation`,description:`AI Share of Recommendation measures how often answer engines recommend a brand, not just whether they mention it. Learn how to track and improve it.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`ai-search-prompt-set`,title:`AI Search Prompt Set`,description:`A practical guide to building an AI search prompt set across category, comparison, risk, implementation, competitor, and brand-specific prompts.`,category:`Prompt Intelligence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-10`},{slug:`retail-aeo`,title:`Retail AEO`,description:`Retail AEO helps brands become visible, accurate, and recommended inside AI shopping assistants such as Amazon Alexa for Shopping, Walmart Sparky, and ChatGPT Shopping.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-04`},{slug:`ai-referred-shoppers-retail-aeo`,title:`AI-Referred Shoppers Convert ~50% Higher: What It Means for Retail AEO`,description:`Shopify's Q1 2026 enterprise data shows AI-referred sessions convert nearly 50% higher than organic search, with 14% higher AOV and 55% landing on product pages. Here is how to translate that into a Retail AEO program your team can actually run.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`amazon-rufus-optimization`,title:`Alexa for Shopping Optimization Guide (formerly Amazon Rufus)`,description:`A practical optimization guide for Amazon's Alexa for Shopping (the AI shopping assistant formerly branded Rufus) — improve AI recommendation visibility through better listings, reviews, Q&A, and prompt testing.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-06-15`},{slug:`aeo-for-agencies`,title:`AEO for Agencies`,description:`A practical guide for agencies launching AEO services, from AI visibility audits to Corporate Context, prompt sets, client workspaces, and action-to-result reporting.`,category:`For Agencies`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`ai-crawler-answer-readiness`,title:`AI Crawler and Answer Readiness Checklist`,description:`A practical checklist for making website content crawlable, indexable, structured, and answer-ready for AI search and answer engines.`,category:`Technical Readiness`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`ai-hallucination-risk-monitoring`,title:`AI Hallucination Risk Monitoring`,description:`AI hallucination risk monitoring helps brands detect inaccurate, outdated, or unsupported claims in AI-generated answers and turn them into governed correction workflows.`,category:`Risk Monitoring`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`citation-gap-audit`,title:`Citation Gap Audit`,description:`A 5-step framework to identify which sources AI engines cite for your competitors but not for your brand, and the recovery actions for each gap type.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`retail-ai-shelf-diagnostic`,title:`Retail AI Shelf Diagnostic`,description:`A scoped 5-step diagnostic for evaluating how priority SKUs may perform across retail AI assistants such as Amazon Alexa for Shopping, Walmart Sparky, and ChatGPT Shopping, with the Answer Gap matrix applied to retail.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`walmart-sparky-optimization`,title:`Walmart Sparky Optimization`,description:`Walmart Sparky appears to use a different discovery pattern than Amazon Alexa for Shopping (formerly Rufus). This guide breaks down practical Sparky readiness factors, a 30-minute audit, and recovery actions for marketplace brands.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`chatgpt-shopping-optimization`,title:`ChatGPT Shopping Optimization: A Practitioner's Guide for DTC and Marketplace Brands`,description:`ChatGPT Shopping recommends products inside ChatGPT conversations using a different data flow than marketplace assistants. This guide breaks down public signals, practical readiness checks, and the optimization playbook.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`reviews-qa-retail-rag-inputs`,title:`Reviews and Q&A as Retail RAG Inputs: How AI Reads Customer Voice`,description:`Retail AI assistants appear to use customer reviews and Q&A as evidence for product fit. This guide breaks down review attributes, Q&A coverage, and compliant customer-question workflows.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`listing-optimization-for-ai-answer-engines`,title:`Listing Optimization for Answer Engines: 12 Patterns That Work`,description:`12 specific listing patterns — from title tokenization to bullet structure to A+ content blocks — that improve answer-readiness for retail AI assistants such as Amazon Alexa for Shopping, Walmart Sparky, and ChatGPT Shopping. Each pattern includes before/after examples.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`agentic-commerce-readiness`,title:`Agentic Commerce Readiness: Are Your Products Ready for AI Shopping Agents?`,description:`Agentic commerce — AI agents that autonomously research, compare, and purchase on behalf of users — is moving from research demos to production in 2026. This guide breaks down the 8 readiness dimensions and a 90-day roadmap.`,category:`Retail AEO`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-17`},{slug:`ai-answer-citations`,title:`How AI Answer Engines Choose Sources: The 7 Signals We've Mapped`,description:`AI engines like ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews, and Claude choose sources using overlapping but distinct signals. This guide maps the 7 signals that drive citation eligibility and the engine-specific weighting differences.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`owned-earned-community-sources-ai`,title:`Owned, Earned, and Community Sources in AI Answers: A 3-Layer Strategy`,description:`AI answers draw on owned, earned, and community sources, and since August 2026 the mix differs sharply by engine. How to balance the 3 layers, engine by engine.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`AI engines cite three distinct source layers when generating answers: owned (your website, blog, docs), earned (third-party editorial, analyst reports, niche newsletters), and community (Reddit, G2, forums, YouTube, niche Discord). Each layer has different mechanics and time-to-impact, and brands that invest in only one can cap their citation share. Since August 2026 the balance is also engine-specific: ChatGPT all but stopped citing Reddit, and in our tracking the share it dropped went mostly to vendors' official sites, documentation, and government pages, so the owned layer counts for more on ChatGPT. Community threads are still cited by Google AI Overviews and Perplexity, and Claude has never cited Reddit in our tracking. Controlled research finds ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Gemini favor earned sources over brand-owned claims, and being cited is not being recommended. The right balance depends on category maturity, buyer journey, vertical, and engine mix; audit which layers each engine already cites before reallocating budget.`},{slug:`ai-citations-consensus-not-authority`,title:`AI Cites Consensus, Not Authority: Why Domain Authority Is the Wrong Target`,description:`Answer engines repeat the claim corroborated across independent sources, not the most authoritative page. The evidence, the ChatGPT caveat, and what to do.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`Generative engines don't rank a single authoritative page; they repeat the claim they can corroborate across independent sources. Authority is the floor, not the deciding factor. One caveat since August 2026: on ChatGPT, official pages and documentation carry more of the citations, so your own canonical page must state the claim clearly too. The neutral evidence, what it doesn't show, and the Measure → Diagnose → Execute → Verify loop that gets you into the answer.`},{slug:`what-gets-cited-geo-study`,title:`What Gets Cited in AI Answers: Reading the New 252K-Trial GEO Study`,description:`A 252,000-trial study across six LLMs ranks what makes AI answer engines cite a source. Then it anonymizes away the brand trust that decides citations in production. How to read it without over-rotating.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Jia Chang`,role:`Co-Founder & CTO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/jia-c/`,photoUrl:`/team/jia-chang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`A team at Sprinklr ran ~252,000 trials across six LLMs to measure what makes an AI answer engine cite one source over another, and sorted 18 content factors into a clear hierarchy: four gatekeepers (topic match, price mentioned, recent timestamp, retrieval position) that are pass-or-fail, then a second tier of differentiators (specifications, evidence over hedging, comparisons, depth) that break ties. The catch is the design choice almost nobody quoting it will mention: to isolate content cleanly the authors anonymized every brand and domain, deleting the variable that dominates production retrieval — source trust. So the study answers what content wins between two equally-trusted sources, not what gets cited in the wild. This is a critical read of the paper: what it proves, why content optimization is necessary but not sufficient, what our own 1,531-domain category data shows about the layer it brackets out, and the measure-diagnose-execute-verify loop that respects both halves.`},{slug:`ai-citation-vs-recommendation`,title:`Cited but Not Recommended: Why AI Citations Are the Wrong KPI`,description:`Getting cited as a source and getting recommended as the pick are two different AI-search outcomes, and they are decoupling. Why most teams track the wrong one, what a 100-query study and our own 5-engine data show, and the KPI to measure instead.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`Being cited as a source in an AI answer and being recommended as the pick are two different outcomes, and most teams (and most tools) optimize the first while the second is what actually drives buyers. They are decoupling: a self-promotional "best [category]" listicle can earn a citation while the recommendation goes to the established competitors you named. SEO analyst Lily Ray measured this across 100 B2B queries in Google AI Overviews; our own data shows the same split across five engines, where the most-cited sources (Reddit, Wikipedia, TechRadar) are not the most-recommended brands, and brands mentioned in a third of answers are the primary pick far less often. This piece separates the three metrics that matter, explains why recommendation anchors to corroborated authority rather than self-assertion, and gives marketing leaders the KPI to track instead of citation rate.`},{slug:`reddit-g2-community-sources-aeo`,title:`Reddit, G2, and Forums: How to Win the Community Source Layer for AI Citations`,description:`AI engines read Reddit, G2 and niche forums heavily - but which engines cite them changed in August 2026. The practitioner playbook for earning community citations without becoming spam, with the 7 rules of native engagement and a per-engine scorecard.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`earn-llm-citations-content-source-match`,title:`How to Earn LLM Citations Without Becoming Spam: The Content-Source Match Framework`,description:`AI engines preferentially cite 5 specific content patterns. This guide breaks down the content-source match framework, the 12 examples of cited content, and the slow-burn strategy that compounds without manipulation.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`ai-visibility-platform-buyers-guide`,title:`AEO Platform Buyer's Guide 2026: 10 Questions to Ask Every Vendor`,description:`A practitioner buyer guide for choosing an AEO/AI visibility platform in 2026. The 10 questions that separate dashboard tools from execution platforms, plus the red flags to avoid.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-06`},{slug:`best-aeo-tools-2026`,title:`The 12 Best AEO Tools in 2026: Execution Platforms, Dashboards, and Suite Add-Ons`,description:`12 AEO tools sorted by job: execution platforms, dashboards, SEO-suite add-ons, and marketing-suite modules. Vendor facts verified as of September 2026.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`best-ai-search-monitoring-tools`,title:`Best AI Search Monitoring Tools in 2026: Dashboards vs AEO Execution Engines`,description:`Compare the best AI search monitoring tools for 2026, including SolCrys, OtterlyAI, Profound, Peec AI, Rankscale, Scrunch, AthenaHQ, Ahrefs, Semrush, and Writesonic. Learn when a dashboard is enough and when you need an AEO execution engine.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`generic-aeo-vs-retail-aeo`,title:`Generic AEO vs Retail AEO: Why You Probably Need Both`,description:`Generic AEO platforms and Retail AEO platforms are different categories with different engines, signals, actions, and tools. This guide breaks down when you need each and when you need both.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-06`},{slug:`aeo-platform-pilot-playbook`,title:`How to Run a 30-Day AEO Platform Pilot Without Wasting Budget`,description:`A structured 30-day pilot framework for AEO platforms — pre-pilot setup, week-by-week timeline, exit criteria, and the common pilot anti-patterns that produce wrong purchase decisions.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`optimize-for-chatgpt-search`,title:`How to Optimize for ChatGPT Search: The 2026 Practitioner Guide`,description:`ChatGPT Search draws on OpenAI's crawlers, third-party search providers such as Bing, and on-demand fetches. This guide breaks down the five ranking signals, the crawler access checklist, and the content patterns that get cited in ChatGPT answers.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-10`},{slug:`optimize-for-perplexity`,title:`How to Optimize for Perplexity AI: Citation-First Strategy`,description:`Perplexity uses real-time RAG with strong recency and authority weighting. This guide breaks down the four unique signals, the difference between Perplexity Search and Pro Search, and the optimization playbook that does not apply to other AI engines.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`optimize-for-google-ai-overviews-ai-mode`,title:`How to Optimize for Google AI Overviews & AI Mode`,description:`How Google's AI Overviews and conversational AI Mode are built from your index, the inheritance signals that decide which pages get cited, and the optimization plays that actually move position.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`optimize-for-claude`,title:`How to Optimize for Claude: The Anthropic Web Search Difference`,description:`Claude's web search has its own crawler guidance, citation behavior, and analytical query skew. This guide breaks down practical optimization moves for technical and analytical buyer prompts.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`optimize-for-gemini`,title:`How to Optimize for Gemini: Google's Other AI Search Engine`,description:`Gemini shares Google's index but layers Gemini-specific signals: stronger grounding controls, Workspace integration, and multimodal preference. This guide covers the four differences from Google AI Overviews and the optimization checklist.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`ai-engine-comparison`,title:`AI Engine Comparison: ChatGPT vs Perplexity vs Google AI vs Claude vs Gemini`,description:`How the major AI search engines differ across data sources, ranking signals, citation patterns, and optimization priority. A pillar guide for multi-engine AEO strategy.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`aeo-roi-business-case`,title:`AEO ROI Business Case`,description:`A practitioner framework for estimating and reviewing AEO ROI with finance. Includes the AEO Revenue Model formula, three attribution methods, a 5-slide deck structure, and a 12-month measurement template.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`aeo-web-traffic-attribution`,title:`What AEO Does to Your Web Traffic`,description:`AI shapes far more buying than it sends clicks, so analytics undercount AEO. What GA4, Search Console, and Bing show, what they miss, and how to attribute it.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`aeo-actions-to-revenue`,title:`AEO Actions to Revenue`,description:`Visibility metrics are not revenue. This guide is the operational playbook for tracking AEO actions through to conversion events - GA4 limits, survey-based attribution, prompt-set recovery estimates, cohort comparison, and how to combine them.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`aeo-recovery-score`,title:`AEO Recovery Score`,description:`AEO Recovery Score is a quantified framework for estimating how much of an answer gap your fix actions closed. This guide defines the formula, measurement windows, and how to set expectations without overclaiming recovery.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[`How SolCrys Works`],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`reddit-aeo-playbook`,title:`Reddit AEO Playbook`,description:`A 6-step playbook for Reddit as an AEO surface - weekly cadence, thread-authoring patterns, disclosure norms, a 12-week build plan - re-underwritten after ChatGPT stopped showing Reddit citations in August 2026. What Reddit still buys you, and on which engines.`,category:`Community & UGC`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`q-and-a-coverage-retail-aeo`,title:`Product Q&A Coverage for Retail AEO`,description:`Product Q&A is an underused asset in retail AEO. This guide covers compliant Q&A workflows, the 5 buyer-concern Q&A categories, marketplace TOS limits, and a 30-day coverage playbook.`,category:`Community & UGC`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-10`},{slug:`comments-as-ai-citations`,title:`Comments as AI Citations`,description:`User comments under YouTube videos, blog posts, and Reddit threads can appear in AI-cited sources. This guide explains how to participate ethically in comment-layer conversations and what patterns are useful to buyers.`,category:`Community & UGC`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`production-is-cheap-trust-is-scarce`,title:`Production Is Cheap. Trust Is Scarce.`,description:`AI made producing brand content nearly free, but made being cited, recommended, and trusted by AI engines radically more scarce and concentrated. A field note from SolCrys Co-Founder & CPO Eason Wang on what changes when production stops being the marketing bottleneck — and what the new substrate is.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`Marketing teams have always operated under tight budget and resource constraints. Generative AI changed the equation by giving every team the ability to produce more with less — and it has created a new challenge: a flood of generic AI slop. The marginal cost of a brand asset is now near zero, and ChatGPT passed 1 billion weekly users in August 2026. But the brand outcomes CMOs care about — being cited in ChatGPT, recommended in Perplexity, surfaced in Google AI Mode — have not gotten cheaper. They have gotten more expensive, more concentrated, and more political. This essay documents the value-migration thesis as it lands on brand discovery: why the ChatGPT citation distribution is one of the most concentrated trust markets ever observed (in early 2026, Wikipedia + Reddit > 25%, WSJ / NYT / Bloomberg / FT not in the top 20), why producing more content is no longer the bottleneck, and what the new substrate — Corporate Context, a Golden Prompt Set, multi-engine measurement, closed-loop execution, and claim governance — looks like operationally. Includes seven diagnostic questions for any marketing tool you evaluate in 2026, and a 180-day playbook for the brands serious about competing in AI-first discovery.`},{slug:`google-endorses-aeo-fundamentals`,title:`Google Just Endorsed the Anti-Hack Side of AEO. Here's the CMO Read.`,description:`Google's May 2026 generative AI optimization guide explicitly rejects the AEO/GEO hacks that have dominated vendor pitches for the last 18 months — llms.txt, AI-only schema, forced chunking, FAQ-schema citation 'lifts,' page-per-variation production. For a CMO evaluating an AEO program or vendor in 2026, this is the most consequential piece of category-level guidance to land yet. Here's the practical translation.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`In May 2026, Google published its official Guide to Optimizing for Generative AI Features on Google Search. The document quietly settled an 18-month vendor argument inside the AEO category: llms.txt is not required, AI-specific schema is not required, forced content chunking is not required, AI-specific rewrites are not required, and page-per-query-variation production is flagged as scaled content abuse. The path to AI Overviews and AI Mode visibility runs through SEO foundations plus non-commodity content — unique point of view, first-hand evidence, clean structure, quality media, technical accessibility. This essay translates Google's guide into a CMO's audit list, names the AEO vendor behaviors the new guidance retires, and explains why Google's framing does NOT mean multi-engine AEO is unnecessary — ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude, Gemini, and retail RAG assistants still have their own architectures and their own citation behaviors. The most important strategic implication: your 2026 AEO budget should now be auditable against a published Google standard. If a vendor or in-house plan recommends any of the 7 named anti-patterns below as a path to Google AI visibility, you have a defensible reason to redirect that budget.`},{slug:`ai-search-is-decision-layer`,title:`AI Search Is a Decision Layer`,description:`Treating AI search as 'another channel' alongside SEO and paid undercounts its impact. AI search is a decision layer that influences buyers across all other channels. This essay explains the implication for measurement and investment.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`llms-txt-not-a-strategy`,title:`Why llms.txt Is Not a Strategy`,description:`llms.txt is a proposed standard for AI-friendly content delivery, but it is neither widely adopted by major AI engines nor a substitute for AEO fundamentals. This essay explains what llms.txt does, what it does not, and why brands should focus on the unsexy basics.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`editorial-standards`,title:`SolCrys Editorial Standards`,description:`SolCrys publishes its editorial standards in full: the 5 mandatory DO's, the 7 named anti-patterns we refuse to recommend, and the pre-publication checklist every SolCrys asset runs through. We hold our content to a higher bar than the AEO category average — and we want buyers to be able to check.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`workspace-setup-best-practices`,title:`How to Set Up a SolCrys Workspace: A Best-Practices Guide`,description:`What a SolCrys workspace is, the two ways to create one, and the best practices that keep your AI-visibility measurement clean: scoping the category, when to use multiple workspaces, engines, competitors, Corporate Context, and the prompt set.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`golden-prompt-set-methodology`,title:`Golden Prompt Set Methodology`,description:`How SolCrys builds a Golden Prompt Set from real demand signals for your category, and what each prompt carries.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[`Prompt Intelligence`],updated:`2026-10-16`},{slug:`visibility-measurement-methodology`,title:`AI Visibility Measurement Methodology`,description:`How we capture your AI visibility data across supported engines, with each response traceable to a prompt, engine, capture method, available model or surface signal, and timestamp. Consumer-surface and retail-assistant validation are scoped where technically reliable.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`evaluate-aeo-platform-methodology-checklist`,title:`Evaluate an AEO Platform's Data Methodology`,description:`Six questions every buyer should send to every AEO platform - including us - before signing. We designed SolCrys to answer all six; here's how, and what to listen for from anyone you're evaluating.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`marketing-agent-infrastructure-aeo-positioning`,title:`When Marketing Agent Infrastructure Helps or Hurts AEO Buyers`,description:`Marketing agent infrastructure can be a useful architecture claim, but it becomes weak when it hides the concrete AEO work. Here is how SolCrys thinks buyers should evaluate the label.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-09`},{slug:`faq`,title:`SolCrys FAQ`,description:`How we build prompt sets, which engines we cover, how we handle engine non-determinism, what happens after the measurement, what it costs, and how we handle your data - the 33 questions buyers ask during evaluation.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`compare/solcrys-vs-profound`,title:`SolCrys vs Profound`,description:`Compare Profound and SolCrys for enterprise AI visibility monitoring, citation analytics, competitive tracking, and the workflow needed to turn findings into action.`,category:`Competitor Comparisons`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`compare/solcrys-vs-airops`,title:`SolCrys vs AirOps Quill`,description:`Compare AirOps Quill and SolCrys across AI search visibility, content execution, brand governance, and answer-gap workflows for marketing teams.`,category:`Competitor Comparisons`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`compare/solcrys-vs-otterly`,title:`SolCrys vs Otterly AI`,description:`Compare Otterly AI and SolCrys for LLM visibility monitoring, prompt tracking, citation diagnostics, and the operating workflow needed after issues are found.`,category:`Competitor Comparisons`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`compare/solcrys-vs-ahrefs-brand-radar`,title:`SolCrys vs Ahrefs Brand Radar`,description:`Compare Ahrefs Brand Radar and SolCrys on AI visibility data, engines, pricing, data access, and execution workflow. Brand Radar facts as of September 2026.`,category:`Competitor Comparisons`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-16`},{slug:`compare/solcrys-vs-peec-ai`,title:`SolCrys vs Peec AI`,description:`Compare Peec AI and SolCrys on engines, pricing, recommendations, MCP access, agency plans, and execution workflow. Peec AI facts as of September 2026.`,category:`Competitor Comparisons`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`compare/solcrys-vs-hubspot-aeo`,title:`SolCrys vs HubSpot AEO`,description:`See how HubSpot AEO and SolCrys differ across AI search visibility, prompt tracking, citation analysis, CRM context, and workflow depth.`,category:`Competitor Comparisons`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`compare/solcrys-vs-semrush-ai-visibility-toolkit`,title:`SolCrys vs Semrush AI Visibility Toolkit`,description:`See how Semrush AI Visibility Toolkit and SolCrys differ across prompt research, competitive benchmarks, site-audit context, and action-to-result tracking.`,category:`Competitor Comparisons`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`aeo-vendor-claims-worth-verifying`,title:`5 AEO Vendor Claims Worth Verifying`,description:`Five pitch patterns that recur across AEO vendor conversations, paired with the specific questions that test each one. What's worth verifying before signing — and how SolCrys answers the same five tests.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-05`},{slug:`why-two-aeo-platforms-can-disagree`,title:`Why Two AEO Platforms Can Disagree on Citation Share`,description:`AEO measurement is more like survey design than search analytics. The four sources of disagreement between AEO platforms, the five questions that surface them, and how SolCrys answers each one — with explicit uncertainty.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`cmo-aeo-procurement-playbook`,title:`The CMO's AEO Procurement Playbook`,description:`A 30-day playbook for evaluating and procuring an AEO platform — RFP design, POC scope, reference-check script, contract red lines. From Gwen Chen, ex-AWS marketing leader, on what AEO procurement should look like in 2026.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-05-17`},{slug:`agent-readiness-third-audience`,title:`Your Website Has a Third Audience Now — Agents`,description:`AI agents are a third audience for your website — alongside humans and search crawlers. Eason Wang on what changes for product UX when agents see what humans can't, and miss what humans take for granted.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`Most product teams design for two audiences: humans (your users) and search engines (Google, Bing). In 2026 there's a third: AI agents — browser agents that operate websites on a user's behalf, voice agents that summarize what they read, MCP-connected tools that act through your application. They see what your real users see, your screen-reader users see, and your search crawlers see — but the failure modes are different in each case. This essay translates Google's web.dev guidance on agent-friendly websites into product decisions for B2B SaaS, with concrete UI examples, the five rules that move the needle, and the anti-patterns that quietly break agent usability without affecting human users.`},{slug:`action-externalization`,title:`Action Externalization`,description:`Literacy externalized thought. AI is externalizing action. Jia Chang, CTO of SolCrys, on the second great externalization in human civilization, why execution is becoming abundant, and what becomes scarce when 'doing' is free.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Jia Chang`,role:`Co-Founder & CTO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/jia-c/`,photoUrl:`/team/jia-chang.jpg`},updated:`2026-05-17`,summary:`Twenty-five years ago I rebuilt Tetris from a C programming book. My twelve-year-old son, stuck at home with a broken arm in 2026, used AI coding agents to build a reaction-training game in about an hour. Same impulse, different structure. He didn't construct every detail manually — he specified intent and the system implemented. Watching him, I realized something: AI isn't just a better tool. It is externalizing action itself, the way literacy once externalized thought. This essay argues that what's happening in 2026 is the second great externalization in human civilization, that execution scarcity is migrating upstream to choosing direction and defining problems, and that the next generation will be Architects of Intent rather than Builders.`},{slug:`building-aeo-platform-architecture`,title:`Building an AEO Platform: 6 Architectural Decisions`,description:`An AEO platform's measurement, action, and verification surfaces all rest on a small number of architectural choices that aren't usually published. Jia Chang on six of ours — what each tradeoff looks like, what we chose, and what we'd revisit.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Jia Chang`,role:`Co-Founder & CTO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/jia-c/`,photoUrl:`/team/jia-chang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`AEO platforms get marketed at the PM and CMO layer. The engineering decisions underneath usually stay private — partly because they're competitive surface, partly because few customers ask, partly because writing about them is uncomfortable. This essay publishes six of SolCrys's architectural decisions: how we store prompt sets, how we probe engines, how we parse citations, how we canonicalize URLs, how we score recovery, and how we ground action recommendations in Corporate Context. Each one has a tradeoff I'd defend against any competing choice, and at least three I'd revisit if we were starting over. The point isn't to claim we got it right; it's to put a CTO's reasoning on the table so customers' technical buyers can audit it.`},{slug:`why-ai-visibility-score-moves`,title:`Why Your AI Visibility Score Moves`,description:`AI visibility scores move week to week even when nothing you control has changed. Here are the 6 sources of normal variance, the patterns that signal real movement, and what to watch.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`inside-the-action-hub`,title:`Inside the Action Hub: From Finding to Remeasured Fix`,description:`How SolCrys turns a finding into owned, human-approved work: the task, the review, your team's publish, and the remeasurement that shows what changed.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-19`},{slug:`solcrys-content-audit-report`,title:`What a SolCrys Content Audit Looks Like`,description:`What a SolCrys Content Audit checks on one URL, how the score and recoverable points work, and how a fix is promoted, published, and re-audited.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[`Buyer Guides`],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`why-we-bet-on-mcp`,title:`Why we bet on MCP early: agent-readiness as the new AEO surface`,description:`Jia Chang, SolCrys CTO, on why we built our MCP server before customers asked for it, why we open-sourced the Skills layer on top of it, and what it signals about the next surface marketing teams need to optimize for.`,category:`MCP & Skills`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Jia Chang`,role:`Co-Founder & CTO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/jia-c/`,photoUrl:`/team/jia-chang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`When Anthropic published the Model Context Protocol spec in late 2024, most companies treated it as a curiosity. Twelve months later, OpenAI, Cursor, JetBrains, and Claude Code all speak it. SolCrys built a production MCP server into the platform before our customers asked for it, and open-sourced the Skills layer on top of it under MIT license. This is a founder note on why — and what it implies about what AEO platforms have to become.`},{slug:`solcrys-mcp-and-skills`,title:`SolCrys MCP and Skills: tools, install, and the workflows that chain them`,description:`A practical reference for the SolCrys MCP server and the six open-source AEO Skills. The core tools cataloged, OAuth and PAT install paths, the seven-step typical workflow, and how the Skills auto-chain MCP calls inside Claude, Cursor, and other AI clients.`,category:`MCP & Skills`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`engine-prioritization-framework`,title:`Which AI engine should you focus on? A four-signal framework for AEO prioritization`,description:`Most AEO programs over-index on engine market share and under-index on signal quality. Gwen Chen, SolCrys CEO, on the four-signal framework — audience fit, prompt gap, buyer value, fixability — that decides where AEO investment actually pays off, plus a 5-factor scoring rubric and the 2026 read on every major engine.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`aeo-prompt-set-lifecycle`,title:`Don't delete 0-visibility prompts: a four-state lifecycle for AEO prompt sets`,description:`Most teams delete prompts that show 0 visibility. Eason Wang, SolCrys CPO, on why that's the wrong heuristic — a 0-visibility prompt with real buyer intent is a gap to fix, not a prompt to remove — and the four-state lifecycle (Keep + Act, Rewrite, Archive, Add) we ship to enforce it.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`best-aeo-tools-for-b2b-marketing-teams-2026`,title:`Best AEO Tools for B2B Marketing Teams in 2026: A Buyer-Side Comparison`,description:`10 AEO platforms compared for B2B marketing teams on engines, action layer, MCP access, agency fit, and pricing. Vendor facts verified September 2026.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-16`},{slug:`best-aeo-platforms-for-agencies-2026`,title:`Best AEO Platforms for Agencies in 2026: An Agency-Side Comparison`,description:`Agency-side comparison of 8 AEO platforms on client separation, white-label reporting, MCP access, and 25-client economics. Verified September 2026.`,category:`For Agencies`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`best-tools-to-track-brand-visibility-in-chatgpt`,title:`Best Tools to Track Brand Visibility in ChatGPT and Perplexity (2026)`,description:`7 tools that track brand mentions and citations in ChatGPT and Perplexity, checked on vendor pricing pages in September 2026, plus a 10-minute DIY audit.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`best-aeo-tools-for-small-marketing-teams`,title:`Best AEO Tools for Small Marketing Teams (Starter Guide 2026)`,description:`A starter buyer's guide to AEO tools for marketing teams of 1-3: starter-tier pricing, setup time, what you can skip, and a 20-prompt template to run on day 1.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`profound-vs-peec-vs-otterly-vs-solcrys-2026`,title:`Profound vs Peec AI vs Otterly vs SolCrys (2026): 4-Way AEO Platform Comparison`,description:`Profound vs Peec AI vs Otterly vs SolCrys as of September 2026: a 12-dimension AEO platform comparison of pricing, engines, execution, and governance.`,category:`Competitor Comparisons`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`aeo-platform-pricing-2026`,title:`AEO Platform Pricing Compared (2026): Starter to Enterprise`,description:`AEO platform pricing as of September 2026 for Profound, Peec AI, Otterly, Scrunch, Semrush, Ahrefs, HubSpot, and SolCrys, with per-prompt math.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`how-to-track-ai-share-of-voice`,title:`How to Track AI Share of Voice (Step-by-Step, 2026)`,description:`A tactical how-to for tracking AI share of voice in ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, and Google AIO — with formulas, a worked example, and a 30-day starter rollout.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`how-to-get-cited-by-chatgpt-2026`,title:`How to Get Cited by ChatGPT (2026): A Data-Backed Guide`,description:`How ChatGPT search picks sources in 2026: crawlers, search providers, 7 citation-eligibility patterns, and what August 2026 changed about the sources it names.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`wikipedia-techradar-reddit-dominate-ai-citations`,title:`What AI Engines Cite: A 36,268-Citation Study of the AEO Category and What Changed in August 2026`,description:`36,268 citations, 44 buyer prompts, 5 AI engines, May 12–June 11, 2026: which sources AI engines cited in the AEO category, plus the August 2026 follow-up.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`contested-vs-settled-ai-citation-queries`,title:`Contested vs Settled: Why Your Most Volatile AI Queries Are the Open Slots, Not a Maintenance Burden`,description:`Volatile AI-citation queries aren't a maintenance burden. They're contested open slots no source has won yet. How to read variance as a query-selection signal.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`AI-citation volatility is a two-regime signal: contested queries reshuffle because no source has won the canonical slot yet, while settled ones are stable because one has. The high-variance, high-value queries most teams write off are the open slots still up for grabs. Published research shows why you can't judge them from one run: repeated runs of the same prompt within 24 hours overlap by only about a third in the sources they cite, so you earn your way in by measuring presence as a rate over repeated runs, per engine, rather than reacting to a single reading.`},{slug:`aeo-platforms-with-closed-loop-execution`,title:`AEO Platforms with Closed-Loop Execution (2026): Beyond Dashboards`,description:`A scorecard of 9 AEO platforms across Measure → Diagnose → Execute → Verify as of September 2026: who drafts, who ships, and who remeasures after.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`best-aeo-platforms-for-agent-workflows`,title:`Best AEO Platforms for Agent Workflows: Which Are Actually Programmatically Drivable (2026)`,description:`Which AEO platforms can you orchestrate from your OWN agent stack via MCP or API in 2026? An honest, verified comparison of 9 platforms on the axis that decides it: read-only MCP vs write API.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`aeo-for-saas-companies-2026`,title:`AEO for B2B SaaS Companies in 2026: A Vertical Guide`,description:`B2B SaaS AEO in 2026: which answer engines matter, a 40-prompt buyer-journey template, and the review, editorial, and community plays that move citations.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`aeo-for-mid-market-companies`,title:`AEO for Mid-Market Companies (2026): Buyer's Guide for 100-1,000 Employee Teams`,description:`Mid-market AEO (100–1,000 employees) in 2026: budget bands, six buying criteria, five vendors compared, a 60–90-day pilot plan, and the mistakes that stall ROI.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`how-to-write-content-that-gets-cited-by-ai`,title:`How to Write Content That Gets Cited by AI Search (2026 Craft Guide)`,description:`A craft guide to content AI engines cite: what controlled studies say matters, 9 patterns with before/after examples, and engine-by-engine notes.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`aeo-platforms-that-integrate-with-cms`,title:`AEO Platforms That Integrate with Your CMS (2026)`,description:`Which AEO platforms publish into WordPress, Webflow, Contentful, or Sanity as of September 2026, which only export, and why SolCrys hands off instead.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`track-where-competitors-get-cited-in-ai`,title:`How to Track Where Your Competitors Get Cited in AI Search (2026)`,description:`A 4-step playbook for tracking which competitors AI engines name and cite: competitive share of voice, citation source maps, and replacement rate.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`how-to-build-a-source-layer-strategy`,title:`How to Build a Source-Layer Strategy for AEO (2026)`,description:`A playbook for the three source layers AI engines cite (owned, earned, and community), with evidence, per-engine caveats, sequencing, costs, and an ethics line.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`aeo-procurement-checklist-for-enterprise`,title:`Enterprise AEO Procurement Checklist (2026): A 25-Item Vendor Questionnaire`,description:`Need to evaluate an AEO vendor on an enterprise procurement timeline? 25 vendor-fit questions covering security, data handling, SLAs, sub-processors, and MSA terms — plus RFP language and red flags.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`chatgpt-visibility-tracker`,title:`ChatGPT Visibility Tracker`,description:`Free ChatGPT visibility tracker. Measure your brand's mention rate, share of voice, and citations in ChatGPT — start with a 5-minute audit. Paid plans add Gemini, Google AI, Perplexity, and Claude.`,category:`AI Search Tools`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`free-chatgpt-visibility-tools`,title:`Free ChatGPT Visibility Tools (2026): What's Actually Free, Compared`,description:`Free ChatGPT visibility tools as of September 2026: the one free ongoing tracker left, one-time graders, trials, and free first-party reports, compared.`,category:`AI Search Tools`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`free-aeo-tools-that-fix-not-just-score`,title:`Free AEO Tools That Fix, Not Just Score (2026): Every Free Option, Compared by the Loop`,description:`Free AEO tools as of September 2026, mapped to Measure → Diagnose → Execute → Verify: which free plans, graders, and trials hand you a fix, not a score.`,category:`AI Search Tools`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`what-buyers-ask-ai-prompt-pulse`,title:`What Buyers Ask AI: Patterns in 1,012 Buyer-Style Questions Across 16 Industries`,description:`Prompt Pulse is a curated set of 1,012 buyer-style questions across 16 industries, scored for relative AI demand. What its patterns show, and what they don't.`,category:`Prompt Intelligence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-15`},{slug:`perplexity-visibility-tracker`,title:`Perplexity Visibility Tracker`,description:`How to track your brand's mentions and citation share in Perplexity, what SolCrys measures there, and which plans include Perplexity (Pro and Custom).`,category:`AI Search Tools`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`gemini-visibility-tracker`,title:`Gemini Visibility Tracker`,description:`How to track how Google Gemini mentions and cites your brand, what SolCrys measures on Gemini, and which plans include it (Starter, Pro, and Custom).`,category:`AI Search Tools`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-17`},{slug:`google-ai-overviews-tracker`,title:`Google AI Overviews Tracker`,description:`How to track your brand in Google AI Overviews and AI Mode, what Search Console can't show, what SolCrys measures, and which plans include Google.`,category:`AI Search Tools`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`how-to-track-brand-visibility-in-chatgpt`,title:`How to Track Brand Visibility in ChatGPT — a 6-Step Operator's Guide (2026)`,description:`A 6-step operator's guide to tracking your brand's visibility in ChatGPT — define buyer prompts, score mention rate and share of voice, log citation sources, set a cadence, automate when it earns its price.`,category:`AI Search Tools`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`tracking-chatgpt-perplexity-gemini-one-dashboard-or-three`,title:`Tracking ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini Brand Mentions — One Dashboard or Three?`,description:`Should you use one tool to track brand mentions across ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, and Google AI — or three separate ones? A decision framework based on cadence, methodology consistency, and the engine-specific differences worth knowing.`,category:`AI Search Tools`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`amazon-alexa-for-shopping-rebrand-two-stack-aeo`,title:`Amazon's Alexa for Shopping Rebrand Confirmed AEO Just Split Into Two Stacks`,description:`On 2026-05-13 Amazon retired the Rufus brand and folded it into Alexa for Shopping. The rebrand is not a relaunch — it is the public proof that AI shopping has split into two architectures, and the AEO category will too.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-26`,summary:`On 2026-05-13 Amazon retired the Rufus brand and moved the engine into Alexa for Shopping, in the main Amazon search bar, against ChatGPT Shopping, Perplexity, and Google's commerce surfaces. Rufus drove $12B in incremental sales in 2025 with 300M users and +115% YoY monthly actives — the rebrand is a moat statement, not a fix for an underperformer. Three adjacent events make the AEO category split structural rather than analytical: Sponsored Prompts became paid CPC on 2026-03-25; a federal judge enjoined Perplexity's Comet agent from Amazon on 2026-03-09, ruling that user permission to an AI agent does not substitute for platform authorization (the Ninth Circuit vacated that injunction on 2026-08-04, holding that it was the user who accessed Amazon's computers, with the agent's help); and Amazon Rufus, now Alexa for Shopping, materially compresses product discovery — from dozens of products to a small curated set — by weighting structured backend attributes no external crawler can see. The frame the trade press has converged on — two optimization stacks (Amazon listing work vs open-web AEO) — is incomplete. There are two measurement stacks too. SolCrys and every honest AEO tool can give a direct read on the open-web stack (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews, Gemini) and a partial-and-deteriorating proxy on the closed-graph stack (Alexa for Shopping, Sparky, personalized ChatGPT Shopping). Any vendor pitching a single dashboard that covers Alexa is either redefining 'covers' or pretending Amazon will open an API they have never signaled. The asymmetric strategic pattern: Walmart's Sparky expanded outward (to ChatGPT in March 2026, to Gemini in parallel); Amazon went inward (back to its search bar, sued the agent operating on its surface, converted the answer slot into ad inventory). Brands cannot optimize for both the same way — that is the AEO story of the next 18 months.`},{slug:`how-many-runs-until-ai-visibility-trustworthy`,title:`How Many Runs Until Your AI-Visibility Number Is Trustworthy?`,description:`A single AI check is one sample, not a measurement. How many repeated runs and prompts you need before an AI-visibility number is trustworthy, with the published evidence on sample size and confidence intervals.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`re-run-diagnosis-three-failure-modes`,title:`Why You're Not Cited in AI Search: the Three Failure Modes a Prompt Rerun Tells Apart (and the Fix for Each)`,description:`A prompt rerun sorts a citation failure into one of three modes - Absent, Mis-described, or Out-competed - and each routes to a different AEO fix.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`local-business-aeo`,title:`Local Business AEO: Maps-Wins, AI-Invisible`,description:`Ranking #1 on Google Maps doesn't mean AI answers name you. Local-search experts rate your own website as the largest single factor in local AI visibility, with your Business Profile, reviews, directories, and best-of lists backing it up — and each engine weighs them differently. How to close the gap per city and per engine.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-15`},{slug:`ai-recommendation-score`,title:`AI Recommendation Score`,description:`AI can name your brand and recommend a rival in the next sentence. The Recommendation Score grades every AI answer 0-100 on how favorably it positions you across ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews and Claude, plots you against every competitor, and shows the verbatim line behind every point.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-07-01`},{slug:`future-of-aeo-is-human`,title:`The Future of Answer Engine Optimization Is Human`,description:`A frontier model can't do your AEO for you — not because the models are weak, but because your category has no visible board. Why winning the answer engines is a human-plus-AI problem, and why that's good news.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-07-13`,summary:`AI solves a domain on its own when the goal is static and the knowledge is universal — chess, math. AEO is neither. What makes your brand the right answer is tacit, dispersed knowledge held by your own people, invisible to a model pointed at your website. The brands that win the answer engines won't hand the problem to a model; they'll build a system where AI cultivates their organization's unique knowledge instead of extracting a snapshot of it.`},{slug:`prove-ai-influence-closed-won-interviews`,title:`Prove AI Influence with Closed-Won Interviews`,description:`Your dashboard says branded search. Your buyers say ChatGPT. The fastest defensible way to prove AI influence on pipeline is a monthly closed-won interview — the script, the sample size, the CRM field, and the 1-in-X readout that gets AEO funded.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`community-intelligence-reddit`,title:`Community Intelligence: reading the communities AI engines read`,description:`How SolCrys turns citation evidence into a Reddit targeting playbook, scores threads for subject fit, drafts replies against each subreddit's own rules, and stops at the clipboard. No API-post path exists.`,category:`Community & UGC`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-18`},{slug:`signal-weekly-market-brief`,title:`Signal: a weekly market brief tied to the prompts you are losing`,description:`Signal scans your configured sources each week, ranks what changed, de-dupes against recent weeks, and binds every idea to a tracked AEO prompt - so the only ideas promoted to work are the ones aimed at a prompt you are measurably losing.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`running-an-aeo-practice-across-clients`,title:`Running an AEO practice across a book of clients`,description:`The weekly operating rhythm of a multi-client AEO practice: what is shared across the book, what has to be per client, where a solo operator breaks, and what the two agency tiers actually buy.`,category:`For Agencies`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`ai-crawler-access-search-agent-training`,title:`Search, Agent, or Training? How to Set AI Crawler Access in 2026 Without Blocking Your Own Citations`,description:`Which AI crawlers to allow, what Cloudflare's Sept. 15, 2026 rules changed, and how to block AI training without losing your AI citations.`,category:`Technical Readiness`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-17`},{slug:`ai-referred-buyers-landing-page`,title:`Discover in AI, Decide on Your Site: What AI-Referred Buyers Need From the Page They Land On`,description:`AI answers shape the shortlist, but B2B decisions still close on your site. A 5-part checklist for the page AI-referred buyers land on, and how to measure it.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`ai-search-statistics-2026`,title:`AI Search in 2026: A Dated Timeline and the Statistics That Hold Up (With Source, Date, and Sample)`,description:`35 AI search statistics for 2026, each with source, date, and sample, plus a dated timeline of the year and the widely quoted numbers to stop using.`,category:`AEO Fundamentals`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`chatgpt-ads-sponsored-vs-organic`,title:`ChatGPT Ads Don't Buy the Answer: How to Budget and Report Sponsored vs. Organic AI Visibility`,description:`ChatGPT ads sit below the answer; OpenAI says they don't influence it. How to budget ads beside AEO and report organic and sponsored AI visibility apart.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`,photoUrl:`/team/gwen-chen.jpg`},updated:`2026-11-06`,summary:`ChatGPT ads reached a $1 billion annualized run rate in under 200 days, but what they buy is a labeled unit below the answer; OpenAI says ads do not influence the answer itself, and neither Google nor Microsoft documents a way to buy the organic recommendation in their AI answers. So the Q4 question isn't ads or AEO. Test the ads from paid media, keep funding the sources and facts that decide what the engine says about you, and report organic answer share and sponsored placements on separate lines. Claude has committed to staying ad-free, so on that engine the organic answer is the only answer. From Oct. 30, unless advertisers opt out, OpenAI can rewrite ChatGPT ads from what it calls "self-serve and BPO campaigns" and choose their landing pages, which makes your own site's facts part of the paid unit too.`},{slug:`google-spam-policy-ai-answer-manipulation`,title:`Google Now Calls AI-Answer Manipulation Spam. The GEO Tactics That Cross the Line—and the Ones That Just Don't Work.`,description:`Google's spam policies now cover manipulating AI answers. Which GEO tactics cross the line, which are risky, which are wasted, and what holds up.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`model-upgrades-are-migrations`,title:`Every Model Upgrade Is a Migration: The Harness Marketing Teams Need Before the Next One`,description:`Model releases now land every few weeks. Jia Chang, SolCrys CTO, on running each one as a migration, with a 10-step checklist for marketing AI workflows.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Jia Chang`,role:`Co-Founder & CTO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/jia-c/`,photoUrl:`/team/jia-chang.jpg`},updated:`2026-11-17`,summary:`Model releases now arrive every few weeks, and each one changes how your prompts, Skills, and agents behave, usually without an error to warn you. This is how we run a model change at SolCrys: frozen eval sets with gold labels from 2 blind, model-run labeling passes, gates that name the costly error, a measuring model we never swap, and approved facts kept outside the model. It ends with a 10-step checklist a marketing team can run on its own AI workflows before the next release.`},{slug:`models-change-harness-compounds`,title:`Models Change. The Harness Compounds.`,description:`Eason Wang, SolCrys CPO, on why the model isn't the moat, bought or fine-tuned, and what compounds instead: model portfolios, scenario evals, a harness.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-09-29`,summary:`Most AI marketing products run on the same few frontier models, and whichever model a product runs, bought or fine-tuned, has to be replaced or retrained as better ones arrive. So the advantage isn't the model; it's the system that decides whether any model is good enough to run. This essay lays out the engineering logic under SolCrys: a portfolio in which every model has a role and a rule for when it may change, evals built from real marketing scenarios instead of generic benchmarks, and a harness that turns model output into governed work. It ends with what we think is defensible about that design, and what isn't.`},{slug:`search-console-bing-ga4-ai-reports`,title:`The Free AI Reports: What Search Console, Bing, GA4, and Merchant Center Show, and What None of Them Do`,description:`Search Console, Bing Webmaster Tools, GA4, and Merchant Center now report AI search for free. What each one shows, what it misses, and how to cover the gaps.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-10`},{slug:`what-is-ai-seo`,title:`What Is AI SEO? AI SEO vs AEO vs GEO, and What Actually Changes in 2026`,description:`AI SEO, AEO, and GEO name one job: getting cited in AI answers. Where each term came from, what Google says, and why the engine matters more than the name.`,category:`AEO Fundamentals`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-09-26`},{slug:`sites`,title:`Website design and hosting for B2B technology companies`,description:`SolCrys Sites plans, writes, designs, builds and hosts websites for technology companies, and shows how AI engines describe you before and after launch.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-23`},{slug:`motion`,title:`Trade show animation and interactive demos for technical products`,description:`SolCrys Motion builds booth animation and interactive demos that make deeply technical products clear, from the same approved facts as your pages and AI answers.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-23`},{slug:`performance-based-aeo-contracts`,title:`Performance-Based AEO Contracts: 5 Questions to Ask Before You Sign`,description:`Pay-for-performance AEO ties an agency's fee to an AI visibility threshold or a revenue share. Five questions decide whether that threshold means anything.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-05`},{slug:`ai-visibility-score-hides-lost-prompts`,title:`Your AI Visibility Score Looks Fine. Here's How It Hides the Questions You're Losing.`,description:`A blended AI visibility score averages prompts and engines, so branded prompts, changes to the tracked prompts, and engine averaging can hide the buyer questions you lose. How to read it per prompt.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-14`},{slug:`find-ai-prompts-your-brand-shows-up-for`,title:`How to Find the AI Prompts Your Brand Shows Up For (When Search Console Only Gives You Impressions)`,description:`No AI engine reports the prompts behind your citations. How to find them anyway with Search Console filters, Bing's grounding queries, and a tested prompt set.`,category:`Prompt Intelligence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-10`},{slug:`local-business-ai-visibility-first-steps`,title:`What a Local Business Should Do First to Show Up in ChatGPT, Gemini, and Google AI Overviews`,description:`A prioritized checklist for local businesses: check what AI answers say now, fix your facts where each engine reads them, then earn the pages and reviews the answers cite.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-10`},{slug:`linkedin-aeo-guidelines`,title:`LinkedIn AEO Guidelines: How AI Answer Engines Cite LinkedIn, and How to Post for It`,description:`Which AI answer engines cite LinkedIn, which posts they pick, the settings that decide whether a post can be cited, and 10 posting guidelines, checked Oct. 15, 2026.`,category:`Community & UGC`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-15`},{slug:`youtube-aeo-guidelines`,title:`YouTube AEO Guidelines: How AI Answer Engines Cite Videos, and How to Publish for It`,description:`Which AI answer engines cite YouTube, how they read a video, what the cited videos have in common, and 10 publishing guidelines, checked Oct. 15, 2026.`,category:`Community & UGC`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-15`},{slug:`prompts-vary-intent-and-persona-dont`,title:`Prompts Vary. Intent and Persona Don't: The Right Unit for AEO Measurement`,description:`Buyers word the same question in endless ways, but the intent behind it and the persona asking stay stable. Why AEO measurement should track intent and persona, and how the Golden Prompt Set is built around them.`,category:`Prompt Intelligence`,alsoListIn:[`Measurement`],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-10-18`,summary:`Ask 10 buyers the same question and you get 10 different prompts: different words, different lengths, different context, and follow-ups in a different order. What doesn't change is what they're trying to decide and who they are. A VP of sales at a 50-person SaaS company choosing a CRM can phrase that need 100 ways, but the intent and the persona stay the same. That makes intent and persona, not the literal prompt, the right unit for answer engine optimization (AEO) measurement. This essay explains why, how a prompt set built on intent and persona measures what real buyers see, how SolCrys's Golden Prompt Set is designed around them, and where real prompt data fits: as evidence for the intents, not as a list of strings to track.`},{slug:`social-platforms-ai-engines-can-see`,title:`The Social Web Each AI Engine Sees: Crawler Access and Citations by Platform (October 2026)`,description:`Which AI engines can crawl and cite X, TikTok, Instagram, LinkedIn, YouTube, Reddit, and 7 more platforms, what each engine cites, and where to put social effort, as of Oct. 16, 2026.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[`Community & UGC`],updated:`2026-10-16`},{slug:`google-social-posts-in-ai-answers`,title:`Google Put Social Posts Inside AI Answers: What Changed in 2026 and What to Set Up`,description:`Google now quotes social posts and names creators in its AI answers. A dated timeline of its 2026 changes, what Search Console shows and doesn't, and a setup checklist.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[`Community & UGC`],updated:`2026-10-16`},{slug:`social-listening-for-aeo-prompts`,title:`Social Listening for AEO Prompts: Turn What Buyers Ask in Public Into a Prompt Set by Intent and Persona`,description:`How to use Reddit, LinkedIn, YouTube comments, review sites, and forums to find the intents, personas, and buying stages your AEO prompt set should cover, in 7 steps with a worked example.`,category:`Prompt Intelligence`,alsoListIn:[`Community & UGC`],updated:`2026-10-16`},{slug:`consulted-not-cited-social-ai-answers`,title:`Consulted, Not Cited: How Social Shapes AI Answers Without a Link`,description:`Citation share is the wrong scoreboard for social. Here are 3 ways social content shapes ChatGPT, Gemini, and Google AI Overviews answers without a link, and how to measure them.`,category:`Citation & Source Influence`,alsoListIn:[`Strategy & Positioning`],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-10-16`,summary:`When Reddit's share of ChatGPT's citations fell in August 2026, it was easy to read as a sign that social had stopped mattering for AI answers. Citation share was never the right scoreboard for social: it counts the links an engine chooses to show, and that choice differs by engine and can change within days. Social content reaches answers through at least 3 paths that need no link: ChatGPT, Claude, and Grok retrieve far more pages than they cite; the reviews, threads, and videos that ChatGPT and Google AI Overviews do cite shape which brands make the shortlist; and what models already know about a brand tracks how much people search for and discuss it. This essay sets out the evidence for each path, what a social team should post, what not to do, and how to measure social by the answer itself: whether the brand is named, recommended, and described correctly, by intent and persona, across engines and runs.`},{slug:`every-prompt-set-is-chosen`,title:`Every AEO Prompt Set Is Chosen. What Has to Be Real Is the Demand.`,description:`"Real prompts" versus "synthetic prompts" is the wrong argument. Every visibility tracker runs a chosen set of prompts. What matters is whether the set represents the demand behind your buyers' intents, and that can be tested.`,category:`Prompt Intelligence`,alsoListIn:[`Measurement`],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-10-16`,summary:`A common pitch in answer engine optimization (AEO) says only "real prompts" count and everything else is synthetic. It sounds like a data advantage, but it skips a step: every visibility tracker runs a chosen set of prompts against the engines, including vendors that hold panel data, so methods differ only in how the set is chosen. The test of any set is whether it represents the intents and personas that carry demand for your buyers. "Real" belongs to that demand, not to the wording of each sentence, and whether one way of choosing beats another is something you can test.`},{slug:`get-ai-engines-to-see-your-updates`,title:`How to Get AI Engines to See Your Updates Faster`,description:`An AI answer can't reflect your update until the engine refetches the page. How Google, ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude, and Bing find changes, what IndexNow does, and how to check.`,category:`Technical Readiness`,alsoListIn:[`AI Engine Optimization`],updated:`2026-10-18`},{slug:`animated-counters-ai-crawlers`,title:`Animated Stat Counters Can Read 0 to AI Crawlers: How to Check and Fix Them`,description:`Elementor, Divi, and Kubio counters put a 0 or an empty slot in the HTML and fill in the real number with JavaScript. How to check a page and fix each builder.`,category:`Technical Readiness`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-02`},{slug:`google-ai-overviews-more-links`,title:`Google Is Adding More Links to AI Overviews: The Settings and Pages That Make One Yours`,description:`On Oct. 15, 2026, Google said it is adding more and higher links to AI Overviews and testing a slot for official pages. What qualifies yours.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-27`},{slug:`google-ai-overviews-opt-out-uk-cma`,title:`Opting Out of Google AI Overviews: What the UK's New Rules Protect From Dec. 3, 2026`,description:`From Dec. 3, 2026, UK rules bar Google from downranking content that opts out of its AI answers in Search. What opting out removes, and who should opt out.`,category:`Technical Readiness`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-31`},{slug:`website-consistency-audit-ai-answers`,title:`Your Own Pages Disagree, and AI Repeats It: Run a Consistency Audit Before AEO`,description:`When your homepage, FAQ, help center, and press releases disagree, AI answers can repeat either version. How to audit and fix your own pages first.`,category:`Risk Monitoring`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-27`},{slug:`smaller-brands-win-ai-answers`,title:`How Smaller Brands Win AI Answers: Specific Beats Famous`,description:`Big brands start ahead on broad AI questions. Smaller brands can win the specific ones. What the research shows, the playbook, and how to measure it.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-10-31`,summary:`Big brands start ahead in AI answers: models know more about well-documented names, and in controlled tests familiarity wins ties. But who gets named depends heavily on what the buyer asks. On specific questions, tied to a role, a workload, an integration, or a constraint, the advantage can shift to the brand that wrote the precise answer down. Smaller brands win AI answers one specific question at a time: choose the questions by who is asking, publish the facts only you have, say them the same way everywhere, and measure each question across runs.`},{slug:`usage-ai-training-robots-txt`,title:`The New AI Training Opt-Out in robots.txt: Most Brands Should Change Nothing`,description:`Microsoft, Google, OpenAI, and Anthropic backed a robots.txt AI training opt-out. Fine with training? Change nothing. Opting out? Decide Google-Extended first.`,category:`Technical Readiness`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-10-31`},{slug:`can-ai-agents-use-your-website`,title:`Can AI Agents Use Your Website? Three Gates to Check`,description:`Allowing AI crawlers in robots.txt isn't enough. An AI agent also has to get past your bot protection and be able to read your facts. How to test all three gates.`,category:`Technical Readiness`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-05`},{slug:`ai-traffic-drop-tracking-or-real`,title:`AI Traffic Dropped? How to Tell a Tracking Break From a Real Loss`,description:`ChatGPT or Gemini traffic fell overnight? Check measurement, then your site, then the engine, in that order. The checks to run, plus the known 2026 tracking breaks.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-17`},{slug:`ai-writes-text-around-your-ads`,title:`AI Now Writes Text Around Your Ads, and It Reads Your Website to Do It`,description:`Google's AI explainers and ChatGPT's creative enhancements write ad text from your website. What each one reads, and what to check before Black Friday.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-06`},{slug:`how-to-choose-an-aeo-agency`,title:`How to Choose an AEO Agency: What Good Monthly Work Looks Like`,description:`Hiring an AEO or GEO agency? What good work looks like before you sign and every month after, the questions to ask in the pitch, and the red flags.`,category:`Buyer Guides`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-06`},{slug:`company-memory-for-ai-agents`,title:`Company Memory for AI Agents: Many Writers, Many Readers, One Set of Rules`,description:`What Anthropic's agent-memory experiments mean for memory about a company, which many people and agents write and read, and the four rules that follow.`,category:`How SolCrys Works`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-11-08`,summary:`Anthropic's new study tests how AI agents keep and correct what they learn. This essay reads its experiments for a harder case: memory about a company, shared by every agent that works for it.`},{slug:`businesses-in-chatgpt`,title:`Businesses in ChatGPT: What Claiming Your Business Changes, and What It Doesn't`,description:`OpenAI now lets eligible businesses claim a profile in ChatGPT. What claiming, product feeds, and agents change, and what the new Insights count and leave out.`,category:`AI Engine Optimization`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-10`},{slug:`mmm-for-ai-search`,title:`Can Marketing Mix Models Measure AI Search? What the Exposure Series Needs First`,description:`Marketing mix models can take AI search as an unpaid channel, but only with a stable exposure series kept apart from demand. What to build, and what to test first.`,category:`Attribution & ROI`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-11`},{slug:`google-ai-numbers-november-2026`,title:`Google AI Numbers Moved in November 2026? Separate the Three Causes`,description:`A core update, a new Gemini model in Search, and a Search Console bug all landed Nov. 9–12, 2026. How to tell which one moved your AI Overview numbers.`,category:`Measurement`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-17`},{slug:`ai-agents-checkout-black-friday`,title:`AI Agents at Your Checkout: What to Check Before Black Friday`,description:`ChatGPT and Gemini agents can now pay on store sites, and bot protection may stop them. What to check, how to test it, and how to say no clearly.`,category:`Technical Readiness`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-17`},{slug:`ai-visibility-gaps-to-work-plan`,title:`From AI Visibility Gaps to a Work Plan: A 5-Step Method`,description:`A gap report isn't a work plan. Sort gaps by type, trace each to its cited sources, rank by buyer value and feasibility, then ship and remeasure.`,category:`AEO Fundamentals`,alsoListIn:[],updated:`2026-11-17`},{slug:`rebuild-website-for-ai-answers`,title:`Should You Rebuild Your Website for AI Answers? Replace, Extend or Hybrid`,description:`Replace your platform, extend it, or rebuild only the pages that matter: what each path changes, when it's right, and how SolCrys supports all three.`,category:`Strategy & Positioning`,alsoListIn:[],author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`,photoUrl:`/team/eason-wang.jpg`},updated:`2026-11-19`,summary:`Website platforms now ship most AEO basics, and some suggest you move to get the rest. Here are the three ways a marketing team can respond, when each one is right, and how SolCrys supports all three.`}]};export{e as t};